{"name":"Victorian","hex":"#988f97","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Victorian","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the reign of Queen Victoria from 1837 to 1901, or that period.","Of or relating to the culture or social conditions of that period.\nIn a situation of poverty and social injustice; Dickensian.\n\n Of the style of architecture or furnishings of that period."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person born in or living in the Victorian period, or exhibiting characteristics of that period.","An item of furniture from that period."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Victorian"}],"entries":[{"title":"Victorian era","blocks":[{"text":"In the history of the United Kingdom and the British Empire, the Victorian era was the reign of Queen Victoria, from 20 June 1837 until her death on 22 January 1901, although slightly different definitions are sometimes used. The era followed the Georgian era and preceded the Edwardian era, and its later half overlaps with the first part of the Belle Époque era of continental Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Queen_Victoria_-_Winterhalter_1859.jpg/330px-Queen_Victoria_-_Winterhalter_1859.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victorian_era","wikidata":"Q182688"},{"title":"Victorian architecture","blocks":[{"text":"Victorian architecture is a series of architectural revival styles in the mid-to-late 19th century. Victorian refers to the reign of Queen Victoria (1837–1901), called the Victorian era, during which period the styles known as Victorian were used in construction. However, many elements of what is typically termed \"Victorian\" architecture did not become popular until later in Victoria's reign, roughly from 1850 and later. The styles often included interpretations and eclectic revivals of historic styles (see historicism). The name represents the British and French custom of naming architectural styles for a reigning monarch. Within this naming and classification scheme, it followed Georgian architecture and later Regency architecture and was succeeded by Edwardian architecture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/St_Pancras_Railway_Station_2012-06-23.jpg/330px-St_Pancras_Railway_Station_2012-06-23.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victorian_architecture","wikidata":"Q565165"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B11_038-SCOTIA_REGNUM_cum_insulis_adjacentibus.jpeg/500px-Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B11_038-SCOTIA_REGNUM_cum_insulis_adjacentibus.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B11_038-SCOTIA_REGNUM_cum_insulis_adjacentibus.jpeg/1280px-Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B11_038-SCOTIA_REGNUM_cum_insulis_adjacentibus.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B11_038-SCOTIA_REGNUM_cum_insulis_adjacentibus.jpeg/500px-Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B11_038-SCOTIA_REGNUM_cum_insulis_adjacentibus.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B11_038-SCOTIA_REGNUM_cum_insulis_adjacentibus.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Cardiganshire_Election_ballot_paper_1880.jpg/500px-Cardiganshire_Election_ballot_paper_1880.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Cardiganshire_Election_ballot_paper_1880.jpg/500px-Cardiganshire_Election_ballot_paper_1880.jpg?utm_